    "The one written, signed commitment of the week came from the White House rather than a lab, and it binds nobody. The accord asks each company to partner with an independent external auditor or evaluator and to establish a board committee to receive the auditors' reports; reporting on it establishes that it leaves the choice of auditor with the companies, sets no deadline, does not require the auditor to be named, and carries no stated consequences for non-compliance. Meanwhile three labs shipped frontier models inside three days, one of them released to vetted cyber defenders without the cyber guardrails applied to general releases, and no safety case of the kind OpenAI proposed was published alongside any of them. The Treasury Secretary told executives who want federal guardrails to slow down on their own."
  ],
  changed: "Pacing stopped being only a stated intention: a lab cancelled a frontier release on capability-control grounds and moved a stated share of compute to safety. In the same week it lost three safety researchers and its safety and transparency lead, and the only signed commitment — the White House accord — leaves the auditor unnamed and carries no consequences.",
