  ],
});

// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── regulating-frontier-ai-us
update('regulating-frontier-ai-us', {
  text: [
    "As of 5 October the federal government has produced a renaming order and a voluntary accord, and a state has produced the binding law. Executive Order 14434, \"Inaugurating the Era of Super Intelligence\", was signed 29 September and published in the Federal Register on 2 October; it directs that the executive branch \"shall use the terms 'Super Intelligence' and 'SI' in place of 'Artificial Intelligence' and 'AI' and will not acknowledge the usage of 'Artificial Intelligence' and 'AI' in any applicable setting\", and gives the President's science adviser 60 days to submit proposed legislative language for a federal definition. On 29 September Trump and AI executives signed the White House Accord on Super Intelligence, asking each company to adopt four layers of controls and audits including an independent external auditor and a board committee; Reuters reported it carries no stated consequences for non-compliance, and other reporting established that it leaves the choice of auditors with the companies, sets no deadline, and does not require them to be named. On 4 October Trump announced on Truth Social that the Director of National Intelligence would head a Super Intelligence Force with 120 days to report; no executive order or memorandum establishing it appears in the week's presidential documents.",
    "The enforcement machinery moved without new legislation. A Federal Trade Commission spokesperson confirmed an investigation into OpenAI, Anthropic and other AI companies over the potential dangers posed by their products, with formal demands for information reported as still to be sent. California's attorney general served an investigative subpoena on OpenAI, building on an earlier formal investigation, and said developers that fail to prevent their models from enabling cyberattacks \"can and should be held legally accountable\". Senators Hawley and Murphy announced a bill extending Computer Fraud and Abuse Act liability to agent operators and developers, and the national cyber director said in public that there are legitimate risks while warning that once government is introduced directly into the space \"there is a tendency for government to start to want to adjust the dials directly, and it is difficult to reverse that\".",
    "Congress produced no vote. No committee vote, markup or floor vote on any federal frontier-AI bill occurred, and CNBC reported on 28 September that no House bills are expected to receive a vote until after the midterm election and that the Senate was expected to leave Washington until after it. California signed 13 bills on 30 September, among them the first US ban on firing or disciplining a worker by AI alone, a 90-day technological-displacement notice requirement, workplace neural-data limits and two health-care AI laws, and vetoed three bills including one that would have barred AI from providing therapy. The only measures in this arc with chapter numbers and dates belong to a state."
  ],
  changed: "The federal record gained two presidential documents and an accord with no consequences, plus an AI czar announced on Truth Social with no establishing order. Enforcement moved instead through an FTC inquiry, a California subpoena and a new liability bill, while Congress held no vote and is reported not to be expecting one before the midterms. California enacted the week's only binding law.",
  figures: [
    { date: '2026-10-02', value: '60 days', label: "given to the President's science adviser by Executive Order 14434 to propose statutory language for a federal definition", url: 'https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/full_text/text/2026/10/02/2026-20321.txt' }
  ],
  settle: [
    { match: 'committee vote before the midterms', settled: { date: DATE, text: "No. No committee vote, markup or floor vote on any federal frontier-AI bill occurred in the week of 28 September to 4 October, and CNBC reported on 28 September that no House bills are expected to receive a vote until after the midterm election.", url: 'https://www.cnbc.com/2026/09/28/khanna-ai-safety-bill.html' } }
  ],
